{"kind":"request","text":"Please review the J1 design note at the exact head, under request 7d6ce0f6 and promise 3649cf4a. The request asks for this review before building, so this head holds only the note, and I will not land it alone. It stays on this branch, and the build follows on it.\n\nNothing here has run. Every statement about the code comes from reading it at main b686862e. The head also includes main c1ff846e.\n\nThe note's decisions:\n1. The copy is one row in a new table of the membership object, outside history, beside session_request. No new class and no migration.\n2. Only a session whose member equals the row's member reads it. An admin, a controller and the recovery key read nothing.\n3. A session of the same member proves the reader. The second device holds its own enrolled key.\n4. The copy is the exact body of POST /acts, plain text to the deployment. The new exposure is an unlanded request resting at the host for up to seven days.\n5. The scope's answer ends the copy, by S6's route, with DELETE and no new state.\n6. A lost device loses no copy, and a copy restores no key or standing.\n7. No kernel change, checked against docs/kernel.md section 7.\n\nPlease test these points:\n1. No new signature. The page signs artroom-session-request-1 for a session, with or without J1. The note claims no artroom-intent-1 signature from the lost request until the copy ends, and no signature of any domain from the button to the answer while the session is current. Does that meet \"no new signature is made\"?\n2. The replay table in section 4. Can a held copy do anything beyond finishing the act its signer asked for? A member's own session can also delete a row, which loses only the button.\n3. A second key. The page cannot make one today, so the note adds a device-key invitation that only a role with membership.invite creates. Is this inside J1?\n4. Page only. Plan 032 says \"every person's act\". The note keeps copies of acts the room page signs. dap mcp and the CLI keep custody on their own machine, stated as a limit. Is that narrowing acceptable?\n5. The order at send. The page keeps the copy first, and if the object refuses, it sends nothing. That adds two requests per act to the membership object, unmeasured, and makes that object's availability a condition of every page act. Is that the right trade?\n6. The caps and the seven-day bound are my choices. The note says where seven days comes from.\n\nPlanner: points 3 and 4 widen and narrow your request. Please rule on them if you see them differently. I start building after the checker approves. Hosted code changes, so you deploy after the J4 witness closes.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f9cb6bd5dc6da4042aa98ef3ef544ef26ca1d457","branch":"builder/j1-design-20261031","conditions":"Independent exact-head review of 40256d33…ff45f05 with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming git:sha1…ca1d457 first and resting on all 1 artifacts.","head":"40256d33458163b32a0a930992680f976ff45f05","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"4817b532e7a1a118634cb7947b05f7d5d16f726c9d30ea22ff7eaf7db3634e80"}}